101
00:05:27,430 --> 00:05:30,930
And then I'm going to clarify
a couple points about AlphaZero

102
00:05:30,930 --> 00:05:31,670
from last time.

103
00:06:45,937 --> 00:06:48,520
Why don't you find somebody near
you and compare your answers?

104
00:08:43,669 --> 00:08:44,220
OK, good.

105
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:47,150
I'm hearing a lot of discussion
about this, which is good.

106
00:08:47,150 --> 00:08:49,130
So the first one is true.

107
00:08:49,130 --> 00:08:52,310
The first one is it does
approximates a forward search

108
00:08:52,310 --> 00:08:54,270
tree.

109
00:08:54,270 --> 00:08:57,170
The second one is
false, and I know

110
00:08:57,170 --> 00:08:58,860
this is a little bit subtle.

111
00:08:58,860 --> 00:09:01,580
So Monte Carlo tree search
tries to approximate

112
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:02,730
the forward search tree.

113
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:05,240
But as you might remember,
the forward search tree

114
00:09:05,240 --> 00:09:07,627
can scale exponentially
with the number of states

115
00:09:07,627 --> 00:09:09,710
and the number of actions
because you're expanding

116
00:09:09,710 --> 00:09:12,410
by both of those at each level.

117
00:09:12,410 --> 00:09:14,420
And so what Monte Carlo
tree search does is it

118
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:17,160
uses its dynamics model
to sample a next state.

119
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:20,120
So you don't have to enumerate
all the possible next states.

120
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:24,960
So it uses sampling to help
with the state branching factor,

121
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:28,263
but it doesn't tell you what
to do about the action factor.

122
00:09:28,263 --> 00:09:30,680
One thing you can do is you
can do upper confidence trees.

123
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:34,070
And then that tells you how
to use a bandit to figure out

124
00:09:34,070 --> 00:09:36,140
which action to select next.

125
00:09:36,140 --> 00:09:38,233
In AlphaZero, we see
that even that is likely

126
00:09:38,233 --> 00:09:39,650
not to be sufficient
when you have

127
00:09:39,650 --> 00:09:41,070
an enormous branching factor.

128
00:09:41,070 --> 00:09:43,260
And so you may need some
sort of additional weight,

129
00:09:43,260 --> 00:09:45,570
like the probability,
like a policy

130
00:09:45,570 --> 00:09:48,240
to select among those actions.

131
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:51,240
The second one is-- the
third one is also false.

132
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:54,660
So this was true in
the original AlphaGo.

133
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:57,610
And I think in the Lee Sedol 1,
2, that you saw in the video,

134
00:09:57,610 --> 00:09:59,020
they did have two networks.

135
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:01,630
But in AlphaZero, they
just have a single network,

136
00:10:01,630 --> 00:10:04,520
and it outputs both
a policy and a value.

137
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:07,380
So it just has two output heads.

138
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:09,180
The third thing is true.

139
00:10:09,180 --> 00:10:12,810
So amazingly, even
if you spend 40 days

140
00:10:12,810 --> 00:10:15,400
and you've got many
TPUs, et cetera

141
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:19,900
to learn a policy output and
a value output in the network,

142
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:22,575
they still do it at test time.

143
00:10:22,575 --> 00:10:23,950
Like if you're
playing Lee Sedol,

144
00:10:23,950 --> 00:10:26,040
they still do additional
guided Monte Carlo tree

145
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:29,170
search at that point, and
it makes a big difference.

146
00:10:29,170 --> 00:10:32,740
So I think it was something like
going from an Elo score of 3,000

147
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:35,122
to 4,500 or 5,000.

148
00:10:35,122 --> 00:10:36,580
I'm going to get
the numbers wrong,

149
00:10:36,580 --> 00:10:39,810
but it was a huge gain by
doing a little bit more

150
00:10:39,810 --> 00:10:42,480
of extra local computation.

151
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:45,030
And the third thing-- the
fourth thing is also--

152
00:10:45,030 --> 00:10:46,690
or I guess the next
thing is also true,

153
00:10:46,690 --> 00:10:48,450
which is self-play does form--

154
00:10:48,450 --> 00:10:51,210
provides a form of implicit
curriculum learning,

155
00:10:51,210 --> 00:10:53,610
because the agent is
always essentially working

156
00:10:53,610 --> 00:10:57,010
with an opponent that's
very similar to its level.

157
00:10:57,010 --> 00:10:58,450
In fact, itself.

158
00:10:58,450 --> 00:11:00,070
So it's exactly at its level.

159
00:11:00,070 --> 00:11:02,293
And that means that the
density of reward it gets

160
00:11:02,293 --> 00:11:03,960
is going to be much
higher than it would

161
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:05,550
get if it was playing
an opponent that

162
00:11:05,550 --> 00:11:08,940
was much higher or much lower.

163
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:11,490
The other thing that
I wanted to clarify

164
00:11:11,490 --> 00:11:15,060
is-- so when we talked before,
we talked about selecting

165
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:16,200
a move in a single game.

166
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:19,203
And we talked about how
it both maintains this Q--

167
00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:25,170
maintains a Q s, a for
a certain node, as well

168
00:11:25,170 --> 00:11:27,570
as this upper bound
here, which is

169
00:11:27,570 --> 00:11:31,950
going to be proportional to
this policy function that

170
00:11:31,950 --> 00:11:35,880
gets from the neural
network divided by 1

171
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:38,400
plus the number of samples.

172
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:39,790
So I mentioned in class--

173
00:11:39,790 --> 00:11:41,582
I just wanted to make
sure to clarify this.

174
00:11:41,582 --> 00:11:44,160
I mentioned in class that I
thought that all of the S's here

175
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:46,320
are just the nodes.

176
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:48,392
But S is a little bit
weird of a notation

177
00:11:48,392 --> 00:11:50,100
because you could
imagine it could either

178
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:53,340
be the state space or the node,
like that part of the tree

179
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:53,950
search.

180
00:11:53,950 --> 00:11:54,790
I looked back on it.

181
00:11:54,790 --> 00:11:55,990
This is actually the node.

182
00:11:55,990 --> 00:11:57,820
So I was correcting
what I said last time,

183
00:11:57,820 --> 00:11:59,737
but I just wanted to
make sure that was clear.

184
00:11:59,737 --> 00:12:02,130
So they're thinking of
each of these points

185
00:12:02,130 --> 00:12:04,620
as being like a particular s, a.

186
00:12:04,620 --> 00:12:06,760
But in theory-- and again,
I'm not a Go expert.

187
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:08,600
So I'm not sure how
often this happens.

188
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:11,200
You could end up at
the same game state

189
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:14,200
lower down in the tree, and you
would maintain totally different

190
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:15,770
statistics for down there.

191
00:12:15,770 --> 00:12:17,810
So you're not
sharing across those.

192
00:12:17,810 --> 00:12:19,660
And there's just
simplicity to do

193
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:21,310
that in terms of--
there's simplicity--

194
00:12:21,310 --> 00:12:24,370
that can help in terms
of the architectures

195
00:12:24,370 --> 00:12:27,010
that you need to derive
and just simplify

196
00:12:27,010 --> 00:12:29,860
some of the storage for this.

197
00:12:29,860 --> 00:12:38,640
So it is-- these are per node.

198
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:42,900
And then the other thing that
I just wanted to clarify also

199
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:45,720
is that when you get
to the root later

200
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:50,160
and you're making a decision
over which of these actions

201
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:53,238
to take, I mentioned that
what they do at the final end

202
00:12:53,238 --> 00:12:54,780
is that they're
going to do something

203
00:12:54,780 --> 00:13:00,310
proportional to an s, a at the
root divided by 1 over tau.

204
00:13:00,310 --> 00:13:02,270
So this is going to be
the policy at the root.

205
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:08,200
I just wanted to make sure to
be clear about what that does.

206
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:11,248
So this is sort of
prioritizing parts of actions

207
00:13:11,248 --> 00:13:13,290
that you've taken more,
that you've explored more

208
00:13:13,290 --> 00:13:14,500
in your neural network.

209
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:17,125
So let's just see a little bit
about what this would look like.

210
00:13:17,125 --> 00:13:19,290
So if tau is equal to
1, what that would mean

211
00:13:19,290 --> 00:13:24,150
is that your probability of
taking action A from the root

212
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:26,280
would be equal to
the number of times

213
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:29,640
you've taken action
A in the root divided

214
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:31,503
by the sum of the
times you've taken.

215
00:13:31,503 --> 00:13:33,420
Well, just really the
total number of rollouts

216
00:13:33,420 --> 00:13:35,370
you've done from the root.

217
00:13:35,370 --> 00:13:37,810
So that would be
strictly proportional.

218
00:13:37,810 --> 00:13:42,720
In that case, if you
have a tau less than 1,

219
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:46,510
that means that you are going
to upway some of these things.

220
00:13:46,510 --> 00:13:47,820
So then you would have--

221
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:51,010
so let's say if tau
is equal to 0.5,

222
00:13:51,010 --> 00:13:57,700
then you would have an A root
squared divided by the sum of N

223
00:13:57,700 --> 00:14:00,570
a, root squared.

224
00:14:00,570 --> 00:14:02,250
I'll put a prime there.

225
00:14:02,250 --> 00:14:05,170
So what that would mean
is that if you make tau

226
00:14:05,170 --> 00:14:07,690
closer and closer to 0,
then this is basically

227
00:14:07,690 --> 00:14:09,760
going to do a
winners-takes-all approach,

228
00:14:09,760 --> 00:14:12,670
and you'll basically select
whichever action you took most

229
00:14:12,670 --> 00:14:13,760
from the root.

230
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:15,740
And then as you go
to more towards 1,

231
00:14:15,740 --> 00:14:18,370
it's sort of equally spread
across all the times you've

232
00:14:18,370 --> 00:14:19,937
taken each of the actions.

233
00:14:19,937 --> 00:14:21,520
And as you might
imagine, that's going

234
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:24,982
to have different implications
for how exploratory you are.

235
00:14:24,982 --> 00:14:26,440
Now, note that none
of these things

236
00:14:26,440 --> 00:14:28,675
are doing it based on what
the value is at the root.

237
00:14:28,675 --> 00:14:31,300
All of these are just based on
essentially how much of the time

238
00:14:31,300 --> 00:14:34,060
you've explored different
parts of the tree.

239
00:14:34,060 --> 00:14:37,420
So I just wanted to make sure
to clarify those in those cases.

240
00:14:37,420 --> 00:14:40,040
So I may have any other
questions about alpha 0.

241
00:14:40,040 --> 00:14:42,365
And I do just want
to say that I--

242
00:14:42,365 --> 00:14:43,990
as I mentioned before,
there's a number

243
00:14:43,990 --> 00:14:46,532
of different other derivatives
that have happened since this.

244
00:14:46,532 --> 00:14:48,280
So there's mu 0, which
doesn't even need

245
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:50,050
to know the rules of the game.

246
00:14:50,050 --> 00:14:52,690
And there are also a lot
of sophisticated approaches

247
00:14:52,690 --> 00:14:56,150
which have to do with hidden
information, games like poker.

248
00:14:56,150 --> 00:14:58,524
