1246
01:07:14,870 --> 01:07:16,390
I'm trying to move too quickly.

1247
01:07:16,390 --> 01:07:19,870
If I was taking the log of each
of these as the prioritarian,

1248
01:07:19,870 --> 01:07:24,580
I'd say here, we have--

1249
01:07:24,580 --> 01:07:26,805
this is coming out to 6.

1250
01:07:26,805 --> 01:07:27,680
This is coming out 7.

1251
01:07:27,680 --> 01:07:28,190
That's better.

1252
01:07:28,190 --> 01:07:30,815
If I want to make things as good
as possible for the person who

1253
01:07:30,815 --> 01:07:34,240
ends up worst off, I might
choose this last option,

1254
01:07:34,240 --> 01:07:36,190
even though in
this option, we're

1255
01:07:36,190 --> 01:07:38,690
getting the least
total good, right?

1256
01:07:38,690 --> 01:07:41,200
The person who ends up
worst off is doing better

1257
01:07:41,200 --> 01:07:45,430
than the person who ends up
worst off in the other options.

1258
01:07:45,430 --> 01:07:49,120
So all these options and
more are available to you

1259
01:07:49,120 --> 01:07:51,492
in moral theory.

1260
01:07:51,492 --> 01:07:53,950
You might take a satisficing
version of any of these views,

1261
01:07:53,950 --> 01:07:56,490
instead of trying to
maximize the total good.

1262
01:07:56,490 --> 01:07:58,240
You might think an act
is right if it just

1263
01:07:58,240 --> 01:08:02,650
produces a sufficiently great
sum of good or weighted sum

1264
01:08:02,650 --> 01:08:03,210
of good.

1265
01:08:08,200 --> 01:08:10,840
We haven't even yet touched
deontological views,

1266
01:08:10,840 --> 01:08:14,350
which hold that, even acts
with the best consequences

1267
01:08:14,350 --> 01:08:19,510
can be wrong if they violate
certain moral rules or rights.

1268
01:08:19,510 --> 01:08:24,240
Often, these rules will be
rules like, don't murder anyone,

1269
01:08:24,240 --> 01:08:29,300
don't steal, don't lie,
keep your promises.

1270
01:08:29,300 --> 01:08:32,090
Right, you might think
that an act can't be right

1271
01:08:32,090 --> 01:08:33,689
if it involves
stealing from someone,

1272
01:08:33,689 --> 01:08:36,510
even if it produces
a lot of good.

1273
01:08:36,510 --> 01:08:39,590
This is something that a
view like consequentialism

1274
01:08:39,590 --> 01:08:40,479
might not capture.

1275
01:08:43,268 --> 01:08:45,560
Although you might think that
these rules or rights are

1276
01:08:45,560 --> 01:08:48,779
themselves justified by
their good consequences,

1277
01:08:48,779 --> 01:08:51,109
it would be best if we
accepted rules like this

1278
01:08:51,109 --> 01:08:51,865
and followed them.

1279
01:08:58,670 --> 01:09:01,880
OK, returning to this
problem of paternalism

1280
01:09:01,880 --> 01:09:05,490
that we encountered earlier,
there is another problem here.

1281
01:09:05,490 --> 01:09:08,399
So one is there's just, what
is the best moral theory?

1282
01:09:08,399 --> 01:09:10,370
Who knows that's--

1283
01:09:10,370 --> 01:09:15,370
I've been working
on that for a decade

1284
01:09:15,370 --> 01:09:17,939
and haven't gotten
much closer to it.

1285
01:09:17,939 --> 01:09:20,064
But even if we knew what
the best moral theory was,

1286
01:09:20,064 --> 01:09:23,710
it might be bad to design AI
agents to act on moral values

1287
01:09:23,710 --> 01:09:25,590
that their users don't share.

1288
01:09:25,590 --> 01:09:29,979
This could be because we want
to avoid a kind of paternalism

1289
01:09:29,979 --> 01:09:34,215
where we say, no, these are
the correct moral values.

1290
01:09:34,215 --> 01:09:35,840
It could be for more
practical reasons.

1291
01:09:35,840 --> 01:09:38,109
Just the users won't
trust AI agents

1292
01:09:38,109 --> 01:09:43,270
if they disagree with
them about moral matters.

1293
01:09:43,270 --> 01:09:44,120
OK.

1294
01:09:44,120 --> 01:09:45,670
So there's some
difficulty trying

1295
01:09:45,670 --> 01:09:50,100
to align to the best or
the correct moral theory.

1296
01:09:53,140 --> 01:09:54,830
But also, like with
the objective good,

1297
01:09:54,830 --> 01:09:56,538
where there's a lot
of disagreement here,

1298
01:09:56,538 --> 01:09:59,920
there's also quite a lot
of agreement about what is

1299
01:09:59,920 --> 01:10:01,320
the morally right thing to do.

1300
01:10:03,980 --> 01:10:09,177
In simple cases, we all agree
you shouldn't kill people.

1301
01:10:09,177 --> 01:10:10,260
You shouldn't lie to them.

1302
01:10:10,260 --> 01:10:12,680
You shouldn't steal from them.

1303
01:10:12,680 --> 01:10:14,690
So another idea for
aligning to morality

1304
01:10:14,690 --> 01:10:17,630
would just be aligning
AI agents to what

1305
01:10:17,630 --> 01:10:20,660
we might call common sense
or consensus morality.

1306
01:10:20,660 --> 01:10:24,810
Common sense, moral ideas,
that most people agree on.

1307
01:10:24,810 --> 01:10:27,990
Instead of trying to
make AI morally perfect,

1308
01:10:27,990 --> 01:10:30,710
we should just aim to have
it make moral decisions

1309
01:10:30,710 --> 01:10:32,650
like a normal person would.

1310
01:10:35,830 --> 01:10:36,350
Right?

1311
01:10:36,350 --> 01:10:37,975
This view probably
ends up being pretty

1312
01:10:37,975 --> 01:10:39,960
deontological and
satisficing, right?

1313
01:10:39,960 --> 01:10:42,770
Most of us think you
follow certain moral rules,

1314
01:10:42,770 --> 01:10:46,570
you respect other
people's rights,

1315
01:10:46,570 --> 01:10:51,100
then you're not morally
required to do the best you can.

1316
01:10:51,100 --> 01:10:53,980
It's fine to do less
to prioritize yourself

1317
01:10:53,980 --> 01:10:56,235
in some cases, things like that.

1318
01:11:00,090 --> 01:11:03,385
Now, one advantage of
aligning to something

1319
01:11:03,385 --> 01:11:05,010
like common-sense
morality, rather than

1320
01:11:05,010 --> 01:11:08,220
to a particular moral theory,
is that moral theories often

1321
01:11:08,220 --> 01:11:10,920
have surprising implications.

1322
01:11:10,920 --> 01:11:12,460
I know we're just
about out of time,

1323
01:11:12,460 --> 01:11:16,350
so I'll skip to
the chase on these.

1324
01:11:16,350 --> 01:11:19,080
I mean, you can think about the
consequentialist requirement

1325
01:11:19,080 --> 01:11:20,860
to maximize net good.

1326
01:11:20,860 --> 01:11:27,750
I mean, suppose you had an
AI agent that was a surgeon.

1327
01:11:27,750 --> 01:11:31,470
His five patients dying, each
of which needs a different organ

1328
01:11:31,470 --> 01:11:33,630
transplant to save their life.

1329
01:11:33,630 --> 01:11:36,030
Well, if you're thinking
about just maximizing

1330
01:11:36,030 --> 01:11:39,370
the net good subject
to no constraints,

1331
01:11:39,370 --> 01:11:42,990
maybe what you think
is, well, that nurse

1332
01:11:42,990 --> 01:11:46,260
walking by in the hall has
all of the organs that I need.

1333
01:11:46,260 --> 01:11:50,260
Maybe if I just harvest
the organs from the nurse,

1334
01:11:50,260 --> 01:11:53,350
put them in the five
people, save five lives,

1335
01:11:53,350 --> 01:11:55,570
the cost of one five
is greater than one.

1336
01:11:55,570 --> 01:11:57,690
We just maximize the net good.

1337
01:11:57,690 --> 01:12:02,470
That's probably not what you
wanted your surgeon AI to do.

1338
01:12:02,470 --> 01:12:07,990
Think about cases
where you might

1339
01:12:07,990 --> 01:12:11,650
want to break a deontological
rule against lying as well.

1340
01:12:11,650 --> 01:12:14,410
AI agents aligned to a
particular moral theory

1341
01:12:14,410 --> 01:12:17,230
might discover some of these
surprising implications

1342
01:12:17,230 --> 01:12:18,050
before we do.

1343
01:12:18,050 --> 01:12:20,350
And they might discover them
in practice, rather than

1344
01:12:20,350 --> 01:12:22,267
in the philosophy seminar
room, which is where

1345
01:12:22,267 --> 01:12:25,600
we prefer for them to come up.

1346
01:12:25,600 --> 01:12:30,202
So by contrast, aligning
to common-sense morality,

1347
01:12:30,202 --> 01:12:31,660
you might end up
with an agent that

1348
01:12:31,660 --> 01:12:33,910
behaves more predictably,
making moral decisions

1349
01:12:33,910 --> 01:12:37,120
like a regular human.

1350
01:12:37,120 --> 01:12:40,780
It might be unpredictable
in some edge cases, where

1351
01:12:40,780 --> 01:12:44,410
common sense arguably runs out.

1352
01:12:44,410 --> 01:12:47,090
Would an AI aligned to
common-sense morality

1353
01:12:47,090 --> 01:12:51,110
kill one person
to save a million?

1354
01:12:51,110 --> 01:12:52,020
I don't know.

1355
01:12:52,020 --> 01:12:52,760
That's what we--

1356
01:12:52,760 --> 01:12:55,310
We got into moral
theory to try to answer

1357
01:12:55,310 --> 01:12:57,060
hard questions like this.

1358
01:12:57,060 --> 01:12:59,780
If we've just taught the agents
to think about morality like we

1359
01:12:59,780 --> 01:13:02,780
do, it might be as unsure
as we are about what

1360
01:13:02,780 --> 01:13:05,030
to do in a case like this.

1361
01:13:05,030 --> 01:13:06,090
I need to let you go.

1362
01:13:06,090 --> 01:13:11,210
So I'll just leave you with the
thought, how bad would that be?

1363
01:13:11,210 --> 01:13:16,280
How bad would it be if AI was as
unsure about morally hard cases

1364
01:13:16,280 --> 01:13:17,470
as we are?

1365
01:13:20,070 --> 01:13:22,190
OK.

1366
01:13:22,190 --> 01:13:24,590
We've covered that.

1367
01:13:24,590 --> 01:13:27,560
I will let you go to
enjoy your Wednesdays.

1368
01:13:27,560 --> 01:13:29,420
If you are interested
in talking more

1369
01:13:29,420 --> 01:13:32,592
about any of this ethics in
general, feel free to reach out.

1370
01:13:32,592 --> 01:13:33,300
Set up a meeting.

1371
01:13:33,300 --> 01:13:36,110
We can talk more.

1372
01:13:36,110 --> 01:13:39,060
Any questions now
before we depart?

1373
01:13:39,060 --> 01:13:41,570
Or I can stick around for
a few minutes, if folks

1374
01:13:41,570 --> 01:13:44,720
want to talk to me offline.

1375
01:13:44,720 --> 01:13:46,230
OK, great.

1376
01:13:46,230 --> 01:13:48,430
Well, take care.

